@page "/render"

<PageTitle>Render Modes</PageTitle>

<h1>Blazor Render Modes</h1>

<p>This page demonstrates the four supported render modes and links to each mode-specific demo.</p>

<ul>
    <li><a href="/render/auto">InteractiveAuto</a></li>
    <li><a href="/render/server">InteractiveServer</a></li>
    <li><a href="/render/wasm">InteractiveWebAssembly</a></li>
</ul>

<h2>Implementation locations</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Server host registration:</strong> <code>BlazorApp1/Program.cs</code> configures `MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;()` and the supported render modes.</li>
    <li><strong>Client mode pages:</strong> `BlazorApp1.Client/Pages/RenderModes/*.razor` each use `@@rendermode`.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared sample component:</strong> `BlazorApp1.Client/Pages/RenderModes/RenderModeSample.razor` shows interactive behavior.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Note: In this .NET 10 app, the supported `@@rendermode` values are
    <code>InteractiveAuto</code>, <code>InteractiveServer</code>, and <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code>.
</p>

<p>Use these demo pages to compare how Blazor renders the same component under different render modes.</p>

<h2>Inventory page mode choices</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Inventory list:</strong> <code>InteractiveAuto</code> is chosen so the host can select the best interactive runtime and still provide responsive filtering and selection.</li>
    <li><strong>Inventory details:</strong> <code>InteractiveServer</code> is chosen for fresh server-backed detail loading, smaller browser payload, and centralized state.</li>
    <li><strong>Inventory create/edit:</strong> <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code> is chosen for form-heavy user interaction and responsive client-side editing.</li>
</ul>
